WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.523
Welcome to Lesson Five of Renaissance Field Lite's Introduction to

00:00:03.523 --> 00:00:06.725
Quantum Computing. We are keeping the circuit fixed and changing

00:00:06.725 --> 00:00:10.888
only the shot count, so we can see how finite sampling affects a

00:00:10.888 --> 00:00:11.849
measured probability estimate.

00:00:12.229 --> 00:00:16.189
Lesson Four changed the circuit size and studied a three-qubit

00:00:16.189 --> 00:00:19.789
correlation pattern. This lesson returns to one qubit and changes

00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:21.949
only the number of repeated measurements.

00:00:21.949 --> 00:00:24.469
That isolates the statistics of the estimate.

00:00:24.849 --> 00:00:28.847
Our objective is to measure the same equal-superposition circuit at

00:00:28.847 --> 00:00:32.481
three sample sizes, calculate the observed probability of one, and

00:00:32.481 --> 00:00:36.116
compare each estimate with the theoretical value of one half.

00:00:36.496 --> 00:00:40.549
We will compare one hundred, one thousand, and ten thousand shots.

00:00:40.549 --> 00:00:43.865
A shot is one circuit execution followed by measurement.

00:00:43.865 --> 00:00:47.181
More shots provide more samples from the same underlying

00:00:47.181 --> 00:00:47.549
distribution.

00:00:47.929 --> 00:00:52.270
The sample estimate, called p hat of one, is the count of measured

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.274
ones divided by the total shots.

00:00:54.274 --> 00:00:57.947
For a Hadamard applied to zero, the theoretical probability is one

00:00:57.947 --> 00:00:58.281
half.

00:00:58.661 --> 00:01:02.328
To compare sample quality, we calculate absolute error: the distance

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:05.262
between the observed estimate and zero point five.

00:01:05.262 --> 00:01:09.297
A smaller value means the measured frequency landed closer to the

00:01:09.297 --> 00:01:10.030
theoretical probability.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.667
The imports match the previous lessons.

00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:16.805
Seed sixty-seven is fixed for reproducibility, while the shot-count

00:01:16.805 --> 00:01:20.191
list contains one hundred, one thousand, and ten thousand.

00:01:20.191 --> 00:01:22.824
Those are the only changing run parameters.

00:01:23.204 --> 00:01:26.265
The circuit itself is deliberately simple and unchanged.

00:01:26.265 --> 00:01:30.091
One qubit starts at zero, Hadamard prepares equal superposition, and

00:01:30.091 --> 00:01:33.918
measurement converts the quantum result into a classical zero or

00:01:33.918 --> 00:01:34.300
one.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.721
The main function builds the circuit once and prints a structured

00:01:38.721 --> 00:01:42.394
comparison header. Each later row will report the shot count,

00:01:42.394 --> 00:01:45.700
measured ones, observed probability, and absolute error from one

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:46.068
half.

00:01:46.448 --> 00:01:48.649
The loop visits each shot count.

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:52.317
For each sample size, the seeded statevector sampler executes the

00:01:52.317 --> 00:01:54.885
same circuit and returns a count dictionary.

00:01:54.885 --> 00:01:58.554
This keeps the concept clean: sample size changes, circuit logic

00:01:58.554 --> 00:01:59.287
does not.

00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:04.080
The code extracts the count of ones, divides by the requested shots,

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.389
and takes the absolute difference from zero point five.

00:02:07.389 --> 00:02:11.433
The formatted row then exposes every value used in the comparison.

00:02:11.813 --> 00:02:15.382
Before execution, I expect all three estimates near one half.

00:02:15.382 --> 00:02:18.951
The one-hundred-shot result should show the most visible

00:02:18.951 --> 00:02:22.520
fluctuation, and the ten-thousand-shot result should provide the

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:25.018
tightest estimate in this preserved seeded run.

00:02:25.398 --> 00:02:29.565
Now we execute the source. The terminal prints one row per shot

00:02:29.565 --> 00:02:33.037
count, allowing us to compare the count of ones, observed

00:02:33.037 --> 00:02:34.773
probability, and absolute error directly.

00:02:35.153 --> 00:02:38.644
With one hundred shots, forty-four outcomes are one.

00:02:38.644 --> 00:02:42.524
The observed probability is zero point four four, producing an

00:02:42.524 --> 00:02:45.239
absolute error of zero point zero six.

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:47.955
This small sample shows the largest deviation.

00:02:48.335 --> 00:02:52.290
With one thousand shots, four hundred ninety-three outcomes are one.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:56.245
The observed probability is zero point four nine three, and the

00:02:56.245 --> 00:02:59.481
absolute error falls to zero point zero zero seven.

00:02:59.861 --> 00:03:03.317
With ten thousand shots, four thousand nine hundred seventy-one

00:03:03.317 --> 00:03:07.464
outcomes are one. The estimate is zero point four nine seven one,

00:03:07.464 --> 00:03:11.611
leaving an absolute error of only zero point zero zero two nine.

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:15.998
The chart puts the three absolute errors on a logarithmic shot-count

00:03:15.998 --> 00:03:20.006
axis. In this run, the error drops from six hundredths to seven

00:03:20.006 --> 00:03:22.677
thousandths and then to twenty-nine ten-thousandths.

00:03:23.057 --> 00:03:26.788
More shots generally improve statistical precision and make repeated

00:03:26.788 --> 00:03:30.520
estimates steadier. The tradeoff is cost: more circuit executions

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.836
require more simulator time or more hardware access.

00:03:33.836 --> 00:03:37.153
Shot count is therefore an experimental design choice.

00:03:37.533 --> 00:03:41.550
Seed sixty-seven lets us reproduce these exact simulator samples.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:45.166
A different seed could produce different counts while remaining

00:03:45.166 --> 00:03:47.979
consistent with the same one-half probability.

00:03:47.979 --> 00:03:51.595
Reproducibility and statistical certainty are related, but they are

00:03:51.595 --> 00:03:52.399
not identical.

00:03:52.779 --> 00:03:56.796
The acceptance checks pass. Each run uses the requested number of

00:03:56.796 --> 00:04:00.814
shots, all three probabilities remain near one half, and this seeded

00:04:00.814 --> 00:04:04.832
sequence shows a clear reduction in absolute error as the sample

00:04:04.832 --> 00:04:05.197
grows.

00:04:05.577 --> 00:04:09.577
This preserved run shows a clean monotonic improvement, but we

00:04:09.577 --> 00:04:11.977
should state the general rule carefully.

00:04:11.977 --> 00:04:14.777
Larger samples reduce expected fluctuation across repeated

00:04:14.777 --> 00:04:18.778
experiments. They do not guarantee that every larger sample beats

00:04:18.778 --> 00:04:19.978
every smaller sample.

00:04:20.358 --> 00:04:23.339
A practical shot count depends on the task.

00:04:23.339 --> 00:04:27.066
Small samples are useful for quick debugging, one thousand shots

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:30.420
often gives an intuitive teaching distribution, and larger samples

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.656
are justified when tighter precision matters.

00:04:33.036 --> 00:04:36.492
Lesson Six shifts from sampling statistics to framework portability.

00:04:36.492 --> 00:04:40.331
We will express the same quantum idea through two software

00:04:40.331 --> 00:04:43.786
development kits and compare whether the measured pattern remains

00:04:43.786 --> 00:04:44.170
consistent.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:48.398
That completes Lesson Five. The runnable sampling code and measured

00:04:48.398 --> 00:04:52.245
results are linked below. Follow Renaissance Field Lite and Codex

00:04:52.245 --> 00:04:56.093
Sixty-Seven for the full twelve-lesson Quantum Foundations series.
